# lower_bound & upper_bound Cheatsheet

Binary search, prepackaged. Both need a SORTED range and run in O(log n).

## The core difference
```cpp
lower_bound(x)   // first element >= x   (includes x if present)
upper_bound(x)   // first element >  x   (skips x)
```

## Basic usage
```cpp
#include <algorithm>
vector<int> v = {2, 5, 5, 8, 12};

lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), 5);   // -> points to first 5 (index 1)
upper_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), 5);   // -> points past last 5 (index 3)
lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), 6);   // -> points to 8 (first >= 6)
lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), 20);  // -> v.end() (nothing >= 20)
```

The return value is an ITERATOR. Dereference with `*it` to read/write the value.
Get the numeric index by subtracting begin: `it - v.begin()`.

## Counting patterns (subtract iterators)
```cpp
// how many are strictly LESS than x
int less = lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x) - v.begin();

// how many are strictly GREATER than x
int greater = v.end() - upper_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x);

// how many EQUAL x  (the [lower, upper) range)
int equal = upper_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x)
          - lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x);
```

## Extracting a sub-range (not filtering, slicing by cut point)
```cpp
auto it = lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x);
vector<int> lessThanX(v.begin(), it);   // all elements < x
```
This is not a general filter. It exploits the sorted order to find the cut point
in O(log n). For conditions that do not depend on order, use a normal O(n) scan.

## Exists / find first-or-last occurrence
```cpp
// does x exist?
auto it = lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x);
bool exists = (it != v.end() && *it == x);

// first index of x  (or -1)
int first = (it != v.end() && *it == x) ? it - v.begin() : -1;

// last index of x  (or -1)
auto up = upper_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x);
int last = (up != v.begin() && *(up - 1) == x) ? (up - 1) - v.begin() : -1;
```

## On a map or set (member versions, also O(log n))
```cpp
map<int,double> m;
auto it = m.lower_bound(key);   // first entry with key >= key
// use m.lower_bound / m.upper_bound, NOT std::lower_bound, on tree containers
```

## When it does NOT apply
- Range is not sorted (result is undefined).
- The decision rule is not a simple >= or > comparison.
  Peak finding (compare with neighbor) and rotated-array search need a custom
  binary search with their own decision rule. The library version cannot express
  those conditions.

## Rule of thumb
Binary search is the MEANS (a step inside a bigger algorithm) -> use the library.
Binary search is the END (they want you to implement it) -> write it by hand.

